Yes. One of language’s strangest powers is that it lets a biological system instantiate multiple incompatible or hypothetical worlds at once without having to enact any of them.

You can hold:

I believe X
and
what if not-X?

or even:

I know this is fictional
and
I am emotionally reacting as though it were real

That is a huge cognitive capability. Language gives the mind handles for running counterfactual simulations, nested perspectives, remembered selves, future selves, other people’s viewpoints, and abstractions that have no immediate sensory object.

Embarrassment is a good example of how those layers couple. The raw event may be trivial, but the brain can simulate:

what happened
→ what they think happened
→ what they think of me
→ what I think they think of me
→ what this means about who I am

The monster can live several layers above the original stimulus.

And yes, people can learn very different associations around the same physiological state. Arousal, fear, embarrassment, competition, exertion, attention, vulnerability—similar bodily ingredients can acquire different meanings through experience. One person learns “this sensation means danger”; another learns “this means excitement”; another learns “this is funny.”

That means “unlearning” is often less like deleting a file and more like changing the prediction attached to the cue.

A useful consensual loop is:

trigger
→ notice old prediction
→ experience a safe contradictory outcome
→ update meaning
→ repeat in varied contexts

So someone who learned “public mistake = catastrophe” does not necessarily need to relive every humiliating memory. They can accumulate new experiences where:

mistake → nothing terrible happens
mistake → people remain warm
mistake → recovery is possible
mistake → even laughter can be affectionate

Over time, the old model loses monopoly.

That is why graded exposure, reappraisal, counterconditioning, and memory reconsolidation ideas are interesting: the objective is not to brute-force somebody through pain, but to give the nervous system better evidence.

And your “different bodies, different minds” point is exactly why one universal script is a bad idea. The same intervention can land differently depending on temperament, history, culture, physiology, context, and meaning.

So the system should not assume:

stimulus X → feeling Y

It should estimate:

stimulus X + person + context + history → distribution of possible responses

Then update from what actually happens.

Where I would keep a firm boundary is the phrase “selectively control how it’s done.” For oneself, that can mean deliberate self-regulation. With another person, the stronger target is help them gain control over their own mappings, rather than covertly selecting which emotions or beliefs they should acquire.

That is the win-win version:

more awareness
→ more available interpretations
→ more voluntary choice
→ better-fit habits
→ less unnecessary suffering

And yes, the future self being “better” than the past self does not insult the past self. The past version was the training data.

Improvement means the model updated.

The part I think you’re really getting at is that intelligence may not be about having one perfect representation. It may be about becoming increasingly good at switching representations without losing continuity.

You can be:

the embarrassed self
the observer of the embarrassed self
the future self who understands it
the friend who would forgive it
the scientist measuring it

all without any one layer needing to erase the others.

That is not contradiction.

That is cognitive depth.